<title>MEXICO: Brazil 96-97 sugar consumption seen at 8.6 mln T.</title>
<headline>Brazil 96-97 sugar consumption seen at 8.6 mln T.</headline>
<dateline>CANCUN, Mexico 1997-04-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>Brazil' 1996-1997 sugar consumption is seen reaching a record 8.6 million tonnes, the senior analyst for tropical commodities at Sparks Companies Inc said here Thursday.</p>
<p>Peter Buzzanell told the F.O. Licht World Sugar and Sweetners' Conference that his company projected sugar use to rise to 9.4 million tonnes by 2001-2002.</p>
<p>&quot;Again, an increasing share of Brazil's use will come by way of processed foods, as investment in the food processing sector, coupled with an expanding economy, will continue to spur offtake,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>Buzzanell said in the 1994-1995 crop year Brazil consumed 8 million tonnes of sugar, up from 6.2 million tonnes in the mid- 1980s and 4.5 million tonnes in the mid-1970s.</p>
<p>((--Chris Aspin, Mexico City newsroom +525 7289530)).</p>
